window.EDUVERSE_BANK_IELTS = {"texts":[{"id":1,"teks":"Bacaan 1: Seed Libraries: Borrowing Plants Instead of Books\n\nMost people visit a public library to borrow a book. In a growing number of towns, however, visitors can also borrow something that grows: seeds. A seed library is a collection of packets of seeds, usually kept in a cabinet or a set of drawers inside a library building, that anyone with a library card can take home, plant, and later replace with seeds saved from the plants they grew. The idea is simple, but it brings together gardening, local history, and community learning in a way that few other library services do.\n\nBorrowing seeds works differently from borrowing books. A reader returns the same book that was borrowed. A gardener, by contrast, returns different seeds: the offspring of the plants that grew from the borrowed packet. In spring, a borrower chooses a packet, often of tomatoes, beans, lettuce, or herbs, and plants the seeds at home. During the summer, the gardener lets one or two of the healthiest plants flower and form seeds instead of harvesting every fruit or leaf for food. In autumn, the gardener dries those seeds, places them in a clean envelope, and writes the variety name and the date on the outside. The envelope is then returned to the library, where another borrower can take it the following spring. There is usually no charge, and there are no fines for returning seeds late, because the system depends on trust and on the seasons rather than on strict deadlines.\n\nMany seed libraries give special attention to heirloom varieties. These are older types of plants that have been passed down through generations of gardeners, often because of their flavour, colour, or ability to grow well in local conditions, rather than because they travel well in lorries or stay fresh for a long time on shop shelves. A tomato that tastes excellent but bruises easily, for example, may never reach a supermarket, yet it can thrive in a home garden a short walk from where it was grown. By keeping such varieties in circulation, seed libraries help to protect genetic diversity, a term that simply means the range of different plant types and characteristics within a species. When only a few commercial varieties are grown everywhere, that range narrows, and a single disease or spell of bad weather can damage a much larger share of the harvest.\n\nThe town of Millfield offers one illustration of how a seed library can develop. Its library began in 2019 with forty packets, donated by members of a local gardening club, stored in a single wooden box. Within three years, the collection had grown to more than three hundred packets, and about one hundred and twenty families were borrowing seeds each spring. Volunteers ran short evening sessions on how to save seeds correctly, and local schoolchildren planted a small demonstration garden beside the library, where visitors could see the parent plants before choosing seeds. The librarian in charge reported that borrowers often returned not only seeds but also stories: which variety survived a dry summer, which bean grew best in shade, and which lettuce stayed sweet long after other types had turned bitter.\n\nSeed libraries also face practical difficulties. Not every returned packet contains what its label claims. A gardener may misremember a variety name, or pollen from a neighbouring plant may have fertilised the flowers, so the next generation looks different from the parent. This process, called cross-pollination, happens when pollen moves from one plant to another plant of a related type, carried by wind or insects. To reduce such mistakes, many libraries ask borrowers to return seeds only from healthy plants, to keep different varieties of the same vegetable well apart in the garden, and to write clear labels. Storage matters too. Seeds kept in a warm, damp kitchen drawer may fail to sprout, while seeds stored in a cool, dry, dark place in a sealed container usually remain usable for much longer. For this reason, some libraries include a short printed guide with every packet, explaining how to dry and store seeds and warning borrowers that germination, the process by which a seed begins to grow into a new plant, cannot be promised for every single seed.\n\nSupporters are careful not to overstate what seed libraries can do. They do not replace national seed banks, the large, carefully controlled collections, often kept frozen, that preserve seeds for scientific research and for emergencies. A community cabinet holding a few hundred packets cannot match that scale or security. What it can do, however, is keep local varieties alive through regular use, pass practical knowledge between neighbours, and give people who have never gardened a free, low-risk way to begin. In that sense, the most valuable thing a seed library lends may not be the seed itself, but the confidence to plant it."},{"id":2,"teks":"Bacaan 2: The Case for Free Public Transport\n\nEvery weekday morning, millions of people wait at bus stops and tram platforms, tickets or travel cards ready in their hands. Most accept the fare as a normal part of the journey, as fixed as the timetable itself. This essay argues that cities should abolish fares on buses and trams. The argument is not that public transport costs nothing to run. It is that charging passengers at the door creates costs, delays, and barriers that are larger than the revenue the fares bring in, and that a different way of paying would serve cities better.\n\nThe financial case begins with a simple fact about how transport is already funded. In most cities, ticket sales cover only part of the cost of running buses and trams. The remainder comes from taxes and other public funds. The share of running costs paid for by ticket sales is known as farebox recovery, and in many systems it is well below half. Collecting that share is itself expensive. Ticket machines must be bought and maintained, travel cards must be issued and replaced, inspectors must check tickets, and disputes about unpaid fares must be handled. Each passenger who stops to pay also slows boarding, so vehicles spend longer at stops and arrive less reliably at later ones. Removing fares would remove the machinery of collection and let passengers board through any door, which would make services faster and more predictable without buying a single extra vehicle.\n\nThe second argument concerns fairness. Transport costs fall hardest on people with the lowest incomes, who are also the people most likely to depend on buses and trams for work, school, and medical appointments. A flat fare, the same price for every passenger, takes a larger share of a small income than of a large one, so the present system asks the most from those who have the least. Free public transport would treat buses and trams as a shared public good, like pavements and parks, paid for collectively because everyone benefits from them. Even people who drive would gain. Every journey shifted from a car to a bus removes a vehicle from congested roads, so drivers would face less traffic, shorter journeys, and cleaner air, whether or not they ever board a bus themselves.\n\nCritics raise two serious objections, and both deserve an honest answer. The first is cost: if passengers do not pay, someone else must, and city budgets are already stretched. That is true, but it reframes the question rather than ending the debate. The real question is who pays, not whether anyone pays. The money could come from charges on driving into busy city centres, from levies on parking, or from general taxation, each of which spreads the cost more widely than fares charged only to bus passengers. The second objection is overcrowding. Critics warn that free travel would fill vehicles with people making unnecessary journeys and leave regular passengers unable to board. Overcrowding is a real risk, especially at peak hours, but it is best understood as a sign of demand, not as proof that the idea must fail. A city that removes fares without adding vehicles invites crowding. A city that pairs free fares with more frequent services answers the demand it creates. Crowded peak-hour buses, it should be noted, already exist under the present system of charging fares.\n\nFree fares would also bring environmental and health benefits, although these should not be exaggerated. Each car journey replaced by a bus journey reduces exhaust fumes and traffic noise, and passengers who walk to and from stops gain regular, gentle exercise as part of their daily routine. Yet free fares alone will not empty the roads. People choose cars for comfort, speed, and convenience as well as for cost, and other barriers matter too: infrequent services, routes that do not reach where people need to go, and fears about safety at night. Fares are one barrier among several, and removing them works best as part of a wider effort to make public transport frequent, comfortable, and safe.\n\nFor these reasons, the wisest path is a gradual one. Cities could begin by making travel free for children and older people and for all passengers outside peak hours, then measure what happens to passenger numbers, costs, congestion, and air quality. If the results justify it, free travel could be extended step by step. Such an approach tests the idea with evidence rather than committing everything at once. The principle behind it, however, should be clear from the start. A service that everyone already pays towards through taxes, and from which everyone benefits through clearer roads and cleaner air, should not turn people away at the door for lack of a ticket. The question facing cities is not whether they can afford free public transport. It is whether they can afford the congestion, pollution, and unfairness of the system they already have."},{"id":3,"teks":"Bacaan 3: How a Swarm of Bees Chooses a New Home\n\nWhen a honeybee colony outgrows its hive, a remarkable event takes place. The queen leaves with thousands of worker bees, and the swarm settles in a cluster, often hanging from a tree branch, while it searches for a new home. The cluster is only a temporary camp. The bees carry little food with them, and they must find a suitable cavity, an empty hollow space, within a few days or they will weaken and die. No single bee surveys all the possible homes. The choice emerges from the work of many bees, each inspecting only a small part of the world around the swarm.\n\nThe search is carried out by scout bees, a few hundred older workers whose task is to explore. Scouts fly out in all directions and inspect hollow trees, cracks in rocks, and empty boxes. They judge each possible site against demanding standards. A good home must be large enough to hold the colony and its stored honey, with an entrance small enough to defend against intruders, set high above the ground, dry inside, and sheltered from strong wind. A site that is too small, damp, or open to the weather will be rejected, however close it may be. Each scout visits only a few sites, so the swarm as a whole gathers information that no individual bee possesses.\n\nWhen a scout returns to the cluster, it reports what it found by performing a waggle dance. This is a figure-of-eight pattern of movement on the surface of the cluster, in which the angle and duration of the straight middle section of the dance indicate the direction and distance of the site, while the vigour and length of the performance indicate how good the scout judges the site to be. A scout that found an excellent cavity dances for longer and with greater energy than one that found a poor site. Other scouts watch the dance, then fly out to the advertised site and inspect it for themselves. In this way, a promising site gains visitors, and each new visitor can confirm or correct the first scout's judgement. The dance does not command other bees to agree. It recruits them, in the sense of inviting them to go and see for themselves.\n\nSeveral sites are usually advertised at the same time, and a contest of sorts follows. Scouts that inspected a poor site gradually stop dancing, so support for that site fades. Scouts that inspected a good site keep dancing and recruit more inspectors, so support for that site grows. There is no leader directing this process. The queen does not choose the new home, does not inspect sites, and does not perform the dance. She waits with the swarm and follows it once the decision has been made. The swarm's choice is therefore the result of many independent inspections, not the order of a single bee. What looks from the outside like a group making up its mind is, at closer inspection, a competition between reports, in which the strongest evidence, gathered by the most scouts, wins.\n\nThe decision is settled when a quorum is reached. A quorum, in this context, means the minimum number of scouts gathered at one site at the same time that is needed to trigger action. Researchers have observed that when roughly twenty scouts are present together at a single cavity, the scouts return to the cluster and produce buzzing signals that stir the whole swarm. The clustered bees warm their flight muscles, the swarm lifts from the branch, and the scouts guide it by streaking, flying swiftly back and forth through the flying swarm in the direction of the new home. The swarm follows these guides to the chosen cavity and moves in. The whole process, from leaving the old hive to entering the new one, may take only a few days.\n\nThe system usually works well, but it is not perfect. A swarm may settle for an adequate home rather than the best available one, especially when food is running low and time is short. This illustrates a trade-off between speed and accuracy: a faster decision risks a poorer choice, while a slower decision risks running out of food before any choice is made. Scientists study this behaviour because it shows how a group with no central control can reach a sound decision through simple rules and independent checks. Each scout follows a local rule, dance more strongly for a better site and inspect advertised sites before supporting them, and the group as a whole reaches a decision that no individual bee could have reached alone. The bees do not vote, and they do not hold meetings. They gather evidence, share it, check it, and act when enough of them agree. For a swarm hanging from a branch with only days to live, that is enough."}],"questions":[{"id":"IELR01","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","opsi":["To argue that public libraries should stop lending books and lend only seeds.","To compare the size of national seed banks with the size of supermarkets.","To explain how seed libraries work and why they matter to a community.","To describe the history of farming in the town of Millfield.","To warn gardeners against saving seeds from the plants they grow."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menjelaskan cara kerja perpustakaan benih dari musim ke musim, manfaatnya bagi keragaman tanaman dan warga, serta kesulitan praktisnya, lalu menutup dengan penilaian yang seimbang. Bacaan tidak menganjurkan penghentian peminjaman buku, tidak membandingkan ukuran bank benih dengan supermarket, tidak mengisahkan sejarah pertanian, dan tidak melarang penyimpanan benih."},{"id":"IELR02","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"According to the passage, borrowing seeds differs from borrowing books because","opsi":["borrowers must pay a fine whenever they return seeds after the autumn.","seed packets may only be borrowed during the autumn months.","only librarians are allowed to plant the borrowed seeds at home.","seed packets must stay inside the library building at all times.","borrowers return new seeds grown from the borrowed packet, not the same item they took home."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan pembaca mengembalikan buku yang sama dengan yang dipinjam, sedangkan pekebun mengembalikan benih yang berbeda, yaitu keturunan tanaman yang tumbuh dari paket yang dipinjam. Bacaan menyatakan peminjaman biasanya tanpa biaya dan tanpa denda keterlambatan, paket dipilih pada musim semi, dan peminjam menanam benih di rumah."},{"id":"IELR03","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"According to the passage, what does a gardener do during the summer?","opsi":["Lets one or two of the healthiest plants flower and form seeds instead of harvesting every fruit or leaf for food.","Dries the saved seeds and writes the variety name on an envelope.","Buys new seed packets from the local gardening club.","Plants a demonstration garden beside the library building.","Returns the envelope to the library for another borrower to take."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menempatkan kegiatan membiarkan satu atau dua tanaman tersehat berbunga dan membentuk benih pada musim panas. Pengeringan benih dan penulisan label terjadi pada musim gugur, kebun percontohan ditanam oleh anak sekolah, dan pengembalian amplop dilakukan setelah benih dikeringkan."},{"id":"IELR04","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"Heirloom varieties are valued mainly because of their","opsi":["ability to travel long distances in lorries without damage.","capacity to stay fresh for a long time on shop shelves.","tendency to produce the largest harvests of all varieties.","flavour, colour, or ability to grow well in local conditions.","resistance to every known plant disease."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan varietas pusaka diwariskan antargenerasi pekebun karena rasa, warna, atau kemampuannya tumbuh baik dalam kondisi setempat, bukan karena tahan dibawa dalam kendaraan atau tahan lama di rak toko. Bacaan tidak mengklaim hasil terbesar atau ketahanan terhadap semua penyakit."},{"id":"IELR05","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"In the passage, the term 'genetic diversity' is used to mean","opsi":["the total number of seed packets stored in a library cabinet.","the range of different plant types and characteristics within a species.","the age of the oldest plant variety kept in circulation.","the size of the harvest produced in a single growing season.","the number of seed libraries operating in one town."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan mendefinisikan istilah itu secara sederhana sebagai rentang beragam jenis dan ciri tanaman dalam satu spesies. Bacaan tidak mengaitkan istilah itu dengan jumlah paket, usia varietas, ukuran panen, atau jumlah perpustakaan."},{"id":"IELR06","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"In Millfield, the seed collection grew","opsi":["from three hundred packets down to forty packets within three years.","from forty packets to one hundred and twenty packets within three years.","from forty packets to more than three hundred packets within a single year.","from one wooden box to three hundred wooden boxes within three years.","from forty packets to more than three hundred packets within three years."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan koleksi Millfield bermula dengan empat puluh paket pada tahun 2019 dan tumbuh menjadi lebih dari tiga ratus paket dalam tiga tahun. Angka seratus dua puluh merujuk pada jumlah keluarga peminjam, bukan jumlah paket, dan jangka waktunya tiga tahun, bukan satu tahun."},{"id":"IELR07","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"In Millfield, local schoolchildren","opsi":["planted a small demonstration garden beside the library.","donated forty packets of seeds to start the collection.","ran short evening sessions on how to save seeds correctly.","reported stories about which varieties survived a dry summer.","stored the first seed packets in a single wooden box."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan anak sekolah setempat menanam kebun percontohan kecil di samping perpustakaan agar pengunjung dapat melihat tanaman induk sebelum memilih benih. Paket awal disumbangkan anggota klub berkebun, sesi malam dijalankan relawan, dan cerita peminjam dilaporkan oleh pustakawan."},{"id":"IELR08","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"Cross-pollination happens when","opsi":["seeds are dried in the sun before being placed in an envelope.","plants are grown in the shade of a neighbouring building.","pollen moves from one plant to another plant of a related type, carried by wind or insects.","pollen remains inside the same flower throughout the season.","seeds are stored in a sealed container in a cool, dark place."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan mendefinisikan penyerbukan silang sebagai perpindahan serbuk sari dari satu tanaman ke tanaman lain yang masih berkerabat, dibawa angin atau serangga, sehingga generasi berikutnya dapat tampak berbeda dari induknya. Penjemuran, penanaman di tempat teduh, dan penyimpanan dalam wadah tertutup bukan definisi proses tersebut."},{"id":"IELR09","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"To reduce mistakes in returned packets, many libraries ask borrowers to","opsi":["return seeds from any plant, whether healthy or diseased.","plant different varieties of the same vegetable close together.","store returned seeds in a warm, damp kitchen drawer.","return seeds only from healthy plants, keep varieties apart, and write clear labels.","return all seeds in spring without writing the variety name."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan perpustakaan meminta peminjam mengembalikan benih hanya dari tanaman sehat, menjaga jarak antarvarietas sejenis di kebun, dan menulis label yang jelas. Bacaan juga memperingatkan bahwa laci dapur yang hangat dan lembap dapat membuat benih gagal tumbuh."},{"id":"IELR10","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"According to the passage, seeds stored in a cool, dry, dark place in a sealed container","opsi":["begin to sprout immediately, even before they are planted.","usually remain usable for much longer than seeds kept in a warm, damp drawer.","are certain to germinate, whatever their age or condition.","need regular exposure to sunlight in order to survive storage.","must be kept frozen, like seeds in a national seed bank."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan membandingkan penyimpanan yang sejuk, kering, dan gelap dalam wadah tertutup dengan laci dapur yang hangat dan lembap, dan menyatakan benih yang disimpan dengan baik biasanya tetap dapat dipakai jauh lebih lama. Bacaan juga memperingatkan bahwa perkecambahan tidak dapat dijanjikan untuk setiap benih, dan penyimpanan beku dikaitkan dengan bank benih nasional, bukan dengan kebun rumah."},{"id":"IELR11","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"The word 'germination' in the passage is closest in meaning to","opsi":["the drying of seeds before they are placed in an envelope.","the labelling of seed packets with a variety name and a date.","the flowering of a fully grown adult plant in summer.","the storage of seeds in a sealed container in a dark place.","the process by which a seed begins to grow into a new plant."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan mendefinisikan istilah itu di dalam kalimatnya sebagai proses benih mulai tumbuh menjadi tanaman baru. Pengeringan, pelabelan, pembungaan tanaman dewasa, dan penyimpanan merupakan kegiatan lain yang disebut dalam bacaan, bukan definisi istilah tersebut."},{"id":"IELR12","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"Why does the writer mention national seed banks?","opsi":["To show the limits of seed libraries by contrasting them with larger, carefully controlled collections.","To argue that community seed libraries should replace national seed banks.","To claim that national seed banks are unnecessary for scientific research.","To suggest that every library should freeze all of its seed packets.","To prove that a community cabinet is more secure than a national collection."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan perpustakaan benih tidak menggantikan bank benih nasional dan lemari komunitas berisi beberapa ratus paket tidak dapat menyamai skala atau keamanan koleksi besar yang dikendalikan dengan hati-hati dan sering disimpan beku. Penyebutan itu membatasi klaim, bukan menggantikan atau merendahkan bank benih nasional."},{"id":"IELR13","subtes":"Reading","teks":1,"soal":"Which statement best reflects the writer's conclusion?","opsi":["Seed libraries guarantee that every seed in every packet will sprout.","Only experienced gardeners gain anything from a seed library.","Seed libraries keep local varieties alive through regular use and give beginners a free, low-risk way to start gardening.","Seed libraries face no practical difficulties of any kind.","The most valuable part of a seed library is the wooden box that holds the packets."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menutup dengan menyatakan perpustakaan benih menjaga varietas lokal tetap hidup melalui pemakaian rutin, mewariskan pengetahuan praktis antar tetangga, dan memberi jalan mulai yang gratis dan berisiko rendah bagi orang yang belum pernah berkebun. Bacaan secara eksplisit menyatakan tidak setiap benih pasti tumbuh dan membahas kesulitan praktis, serta menyatakan hal paling berharga adalah keberanian untuk menanam, bukan wadahnya."},{"id":"IELR14","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"What is the writer's main argument?","opsi":["Public transport should be sold to private companies so that fares can rise freely.","Buses should be replaced by trams on every route in every city.","Only people who drive cars should pay taxes for public transport.","Cities should remove fares on buses and trams because charging at the door causes costs and barriers larger than the revenue it brings in.","Fares should be doubled so that cities can buy more vehicles."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan membuka dengan pernyataan bahwa kota sebaiknya menghapus tarif bus dan trem, bukan karena angkutan umum murah, melainkan karena penagihan di pintu menimbulkan biaya, keterlambatan, dan hambatan yang lebih besar daripada pendapatan tarif. Bacaan tidak mengusulkan privatisasi, penggantian bus, pajak khusus pengemudi, atau kenaikan tarif."},{"id":"IELR15","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"In the passage, 'farebox recovery' means","opsi":["the cost of building new buses and tram vehicles.","the share of running costs that is paid for by ticket sales.","the number of passengers who travel on an average day.","the profit earned by drivers on a busy route.","the tax paid by car owners for road maintenance."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan mendefinisikan istilah itu sebagai bagian dari biaya operasional yang dibayar melalui penjualan tiket, dan menyatakan bagian itu di banyak sistem jauh di bawah setengah. Bacaan tidak mengaitkan istilah itu dengan biaya pembangunan kendaraan, jumlah penumpang, laba pengemudi, atau pajak pemilik mobil."},{"id":"IELR16","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"Collecting fares is expensive because","opsi":["buses use more fuel whenever they stop to collect fares.","drivers must be paid higher wages on routes that charge fares.","ticket machines, travel cards, inspectors, and fare disputes all require money and time.","passengers buy fewer tickets when inspectors are present.","cities build fewer roads when fares are collected."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan merinci biaya penagihan: mesin tiket harus dibeli dan dirawat, kartu perjalanan harus diterbitkan dan diganti, pemeriksa tiket harus memeriksa karcis, dan sengketa tarif tak berbayar harus ditangani. Bacaan tidak menyebut pemakaian bahan bakar, upah pengemudi, penurunan pembelian tiket, atau pembangunan jalan sebagai biaya penagihan."},{"id":"IELR17","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"Removing fares would make services faster because","opsi":["vehicles would spend less time at stops while passengers pay.","buses would carry fewer passengers on every journey.","bus routes would become shorter in every city.","drivers would be allowed to drive faster between stops.","fewer stops would be needed on each route."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan setiap penumpang yang berhenti untuk membayar memperlambat naiknya penumpang, sehingga kendaraan lebih lama di halte dan tiba kurang tepat waktu di halte berikutnya. Penghapusan tarif memungkinkan penumpang naik lewat pintu mana pun, sehingga layanan menjadi lebih cepat dan lebih dapat diprediksi."},{"id":"IELR18","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"The fairness argument in the passage is that a flat fare","opsi":["costs exactly the same, in effect, for every passenger.","is paid mostly by wealthy residents of a city.","affects only car owners who never ride buses.","falls only on tourists visiting the city.","takes a larger share of a small income than of a large one."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan tarif seragam, yaitu harga yang sama bagi setiap penumpang, mengambil bagian yang lebih besar dari penghasilan kecil daripada dari penghasilan besar, sehingga sistem sekarang meminta paling banyak dari mereka yang memiliki paling sedikit. Bacaan juga menyatakan biaya angkutan paling berat bagi warga berpenghasilan rendah yang bergantung pada bus dan trem."},{"id":"IELR19","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"The writer compares free public transport to","opsi":["private taxis that charge each passenger separately.","toll roads that only drivers are allowed to use.","pavements and parks, paid for collectively because everyone benefits from them.","paid parking spaces in busy city centres.","subscription services that charge a monthly fee."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan angkutan umum gratis akan memperlakukan bus dan trem sebagai kebaikan publik bersama, seperti trotoar dan taman, yang dibayar secara kolektif karena semua orang mendapat manfaat darinya. Taksi pribadi, jalan tol, parkir berbayar, dan layanan berlangganan tidak dipakai sebagai perbandingan dalam bacaan."},{"id":"IELR20","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"According to the passage, drivers would benefit from free public transport because","opsi":["drivers would receive free fuel for their own vehicles.","fewer cars on the road would mean less congestion, shorter journeys, and cleaner air.","parking in city centres would become free everywhere.","roads would be widened on every congested route.","buses would stop using roads shared with cars."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan setiap perjalanan yang berpindah dari mobil ke bus mengurangi satu kendaraan di jalan yang padat, sehingga pengemudi menghadapi lalu lintas yang lebih ringan, perjalanan yang lebih singkat, dan udara yang lebih bersih, meski mereka tidak pernah naik bus. Bacaan tidak menyebut bahan bakar gratis, parkir gratis, pelebaran jalan, atau pemisahan jalan."},{"id":"IELR21","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"Which concern raised by critics does the writer accept as serious?","opsi":["Buses will run completely empty once fares are removed.","Nobody will want to travel at all under a free system.","Fares are the only cost involved in running transport.","Free travel must still be paid for by someone, and vehicles may become overcrowded at peak hours.","Drivers will refuse to work if passengers travel free."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyebut dua keberatan serius dari pengkritik: biaya, karena bila penumpang tidak membayar maka pihak lain harus membayar, dan kepadatan, karena perjalanan gratis dapat memenuhi kendaraan hingga penumpang reguler tidak dapat naik. Bacaan tidak menyebut bus kosong, hilangnya minat bepergian, tarif sebagai satu-satunya biaya, atau penolakan pengemudi."},{"id":"IELR22","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"The writer's reply on funding is that","opsi":["the real question is who pays, through driving charges, parking levies, or general taxation, not whether anyone pays.","public transport needs no money at all once fares are removed.","fares should be doubled so that passengers pay even more.","only bus passengers should pay for the whole system.","cities should borrow money without limit to fund transport."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan keberatan biaya merumuskan ulang pertanyaan, bukan mengakhiri perdebatan: pertanyaan sesungguhnya adalah siapa yang membayar, dan uangnya dapat berasal dari pungutan berkendara ke pusat kota yang sibuk, retribusi parkir, atau pajak umum, yang menyebarkan biaya lebih luas daripada tarif yang hanya dibebankan kepada penumpang bus."},{"id":"IELR23","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"On overcrowding, the writer suggests that crowding","opsi":["proves that free fares must always fail in every city.","can only be solved by raising fares even higher.","should be answered by running fewer buses.","means that peak-hour travel should be banned.","shows demand and should be answered with more frequent services."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan kepadatan sebaiknya dipahami sebagai tanda permintaan, bukan sebagai bukti gagasan itu pasti gagal, dan kota yang memasangkan tarif gratis dengan layanan yang lebih sering menjawab permintaan yang ditimbulkannya. Bacaan juga mencatat bus yang padat pada jam sibuk sudah ada di bawah sistem tarif sekarang."},{"id":"IELR24","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"Free fares alone will not empty the roads because","opsi":["buses are physically unable to carry any passengers at all.","people also choose cars for comfort and speed, and other barriers such as frequency, coverage, and night-time safety matter.","car owners would never use a bus under any circumstances.","all roads in a city would be closed to buses.","walking to a bus stop is impossible in every city."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan orang memilih mobil karena kenyamanan, kecepatan, dan kemudahan, bukan hanya karena biaya, dan hambatan lain juga berpengaruh: layanan yang jarang, rute yang tidak menjangkau tujuan, dan kekhawatiran tentang keamanan pada malam hari. Tarif adalah satu hambatan di antara beberapa hambatan."},{"id":"IELR25","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"Which environmental and health benefit is mentioned in the passage?","opsi":["Buses produce no exhaust fumes of any kind at all.","Every passenger becomes an athlete through riding buses.","City air becomes completely clean overnight.","Replacing car journeys with bus journeys can reduce exhaust fumes and noise, and walking to stops adds gentle exercise.","Traffic noise disappears entirely from every street."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan setiap perjalanan mobil yang digantikan perjalanan bus mengurangi asap buangan dan kebisingan lalu lintas, dan penumpang yang berjalan kaki ke dan dari halte memperoleh olahraga ringan yang teratur. Bacaan juga memperingatkan agar manfaat itu tidak dibesar-besarkan, sehingga klaim mutlak tentang udara bersih sepenuhnya atau hilangnya kebisingan tidak sesuai dengan isi bacaan."},{"id":"IELR26","subtes":"Reading","teks":2,"soal":"What approach does the writer recommend?","opsi":["Begin with free travel for children, older people, and off-peak passengers, measure the results, then extend free travel step by step if justified.","Remove all transport funding at once without any measurement.","Make all travel free overnight in every city without testing the idea.","Keep fares for everyone forever and never review the system.","Close bus services during peak hours to avoid crowding."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menganjurkan jalan bertahap: mulai dengan perjalanan gratis bagi anak-anak dan warga lanjut usia serta bagi semua penumpang di luar jam sibuk, lalu ukur pengaruhnya terhadap jumlah penumpang, biaya, kemacetan, dan kualitas udara, dan perluas bila hasilnya membenarkan. Pendekatan itu menguji gagasan dengan bukti, bukan mengikatkan segalanya sekaligus."},{"id":"IELR27","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"What is the main subject of the passage?","opsi":["How the queen bee rules over the whole colony.","How worker bees produce and store honey.","Why bees sting intruders near the hive.","The history of beekeeping in different countries.","How scout bees inspect, advertise, and collectively choose a new nest site."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan mengikuti seluruh proses pemilihan sarang baru: pengintaian, tarian, persaingan antar laporan, tercapainya kuorum, dan perpindahan kawanan. Peran ratu, produksi madu, sengatan, dan sejarah peternakan lebah bukan pokok bahasan bacaan."},{"id":"IELR28","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"According to the passage, a swarm leaves the old hive when","opsi":["winter arrives and food becomes scarce.","the colony has outgrown its hive.","the queen dies and a new queen is needed.","the stored honey runs out during summer.","the scout bees return from their first search."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan membuka dengan menyatakan peristiwa itu terjadi ketika koloni lebah madu telah tumbuh melampaui sarangnya, lalu ratu pergi bersama ribuan lebah pekerja. Bacaan tidak menyebut musim dingin, kematian ratu, habisnya madu, atau kembalinya lebah pengintai sebagai pemicu kepergian kawanan."},{"id":"IELR29","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"The search for a new home is urgent because the swarm","opsi":["can wait for several months on the branch without harm.","builds a permanent hive on the branch where it clusters.","receives enough food from scout bees for many weeks.","carries little food and must find a suitable cavity within a few days.","is fed entirely by the queen while it waits."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan kelompok yang berkumpul di dahan hanya merupakan perkemahan sementara, lebah membawa sedikit makanan, dan mereka harus menemukan rongga yang cocok dalam beberapa hari atau akan melemah dan mati. Bacaan tidak menyatakan kawanan dapat menunggu berbulan-bulan atau diberi makan oleh ratu."},{"id":"IELR30","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"A good nest site is described as having","opsi":["enough space, a small defensible entrance, height above the ground, dryness, and shelter from strong wind.","a large entrance that is easy for intruders to enter.","a position low on the ground near damp soil.","a small interior with a wide, open entrance.","full exposure to strong wind and rain."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan merinci standar yang menuntut: cukup besar untuk menampung koloni dan madu simpanan, pintu masuk kecil yang mudah dipertahankan dari penyusup, terletak tinggi di atas tanah, kering di dalam, dan terlindung dari angin kencang. Tempat yang terlalu kecil, lembap, atau terbuka terhadap cuaca akan ditolak."},{"id":"IELR31","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"The waggle dance communicates","opsi":["the age of the queen and the size of the colony.","the amount of honey stored in the old hive.","the direction, distance, and quality of a possible nest site.","the weather forecast for the following week.","the number of predators near the swarm."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan sudut dan durasi bagian lurus di tengah pola tarian menunjukkan arah dan jarak tempat itu, sedangkan semangat dan panjang penampilan menunjukkan seberapa baik lebah pengintai menilai tempat tersebut. Usia ratu, jumlah madu, ramalan cuaca, dan jumlah pemangsa tidak dikomunikasikan oleh tarian."},{"id":"IELR32","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"A scout that found an excellent cavity will perform a dance that is","opsi":["absent, because excellent sites need no dance at all.","shorter and weaker than the dance for a poor site.","performed only by the queen, not by scout bees.","followed by immediate departure without any inspection.","longer and more energetic than the dance of a scout that found a poor site."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan lebah pengintai yang menemukan rongga yang sangat baik menari lebih lama dan dengan energi yang lebih besar daripada lebah yang menemukan tempat yang buruk. Semangat dan panjang tarian menandakan penilaian kualitas tempat itu."},{"id":"IELR33","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"After watching a waggle dance, other scouts","opsi":["move the whole swarm to the site at once.","fly to the advertised site and inspect it for themselves.","ignore the dance and return to the old hive.","choose the site without ever visiting it.","wait until the queen tells them where to go."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan lebah pengintai lain memperhatikan tarian, lalu terbang ke tempat yang diiklankan dan memeriksanya sendiri, sehingga tempat yang menjanjikan mendapat pengunjung dan setiap pengunjung baru dapat mengonfirmasi atau mengoreksi penilaian lebah pengintai yang datang lebih dahulu."},{"id":"IELR34","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"What is the queen's role in choosing the new home?","opsi":["She does not choose; she waits with the swarm and follows it once the decision has been made.","She inspects every possible site herself before deciding.","She performs the waggle dance to advertise the best site.","She orders the scouts to search in particular directions.","She decides alone while the workers wait for her command."],"kunci":0,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan secara eksplisit bahwa ratu tidak memilih sarang baru, tidak memeriksa tempat, dan tidak menampilkan tarian. Ratu menunggu bersama kawanan dan mengikutinya setelah keputusan dibuat, sehingga pilihan kawanan merupakan hasil banyak pemeriksaan independen, bukan perintah satu lebah."},{"id":"IELR35","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"Support for a poor site fades because","opsi":["the queen forbids any further dancing for that site.","poor sites disappear from the landscape within hours.","scouts advertising it gradually stop dancing, while better sites attract more inspectors.","scouts forget the location of a site instantly.","dances for poor sites become longer and stronger."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan lebah pengintai yang memeriksa tempat yang buruk lambat laun berhenti menari, sehingga dukungan bagi tempat itu memudar, sedangkan lebah pengintai yang memeriksa tempat yang baik terus menari dan merekrut lebih banyak pemeriksa, sehingga dukungan bagi tempat itu tumbuh."},{"id":"IELR36","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"In the passage, a 'quorum' means","opsi":["the total number of bees in the whole swarm.","the distance between the cluster and the new site.","the length of a single waggle dance performance.","the minimum number of scouts gathered at one site at the same time that is needed to trigger action.","the amount of food carried by the swarm."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan mendefinisikan kuorum dalam konteks ini sebagai jumlah minimum lebah pengintai yang berkumpul di satu tempat pada saat yang sama dan dibutuhkan untuk memicu tindakan. Jumlah total kawanan, jarak, panjang tarian, dan jumlah makanan bukan definisi istilah tersebut."},{"id":"IELR37","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"According to the passage, a quorum is reached when about how many scouts are present together at a single cavity?","opsi":["Exactly five scouts.","Roughly twenty scouts.","Roughly two hundred scouts.","Roughly two thousand scouts.","Exactly one hundred scouts."],"kunci":1,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan peneliti mengamati bahwa ketika kira-kira dua puluh lebah pengintai hadir bersama di satu rongga, lebah pengintai kembali ke kelompok dan menghasilkan sinyal dengung yang menggerakkan seluruh kawanan."},{"id":"IELR38","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"After a quorum is reached, the swarm","opsi":["stays on the branch for another week before deciding.","splits permanently into two separate swarms.","sends the queen alone to the new site.","warms its flight muscles, lifts from the branch, and follows scouts flying swiftly through the swarm toward the new home.","builds a new hive on the branch itself."],"kunci":3,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan setelah kuorum tercapai, lebah yang berkumpul menghangatkan otot terbang, kawanan terangkat dari dahan, dan lebah pengintai memandu dengan terbang cepat bolak-balik menembus kawanan yang sedang terbang ke arah sarang baru, lalu kawanan mengikuti pemandu itu ke rongga terpilih dan masuk ke dalamnya."},{"id":"IELR39","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"The trade-off between speed and accuracy means that","opsi":["faster decisions are always better in every situation.","slower decisions carry no risk at all for the swarm.","bees never face any pressure of time when choosing.","accuracy does not matter as long as a choice is made.","a faster decision risks a poorer choice, while a slower decision risks running out of food before any choice is made."],"kunci":4,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan kawanan dapat menerima sarang yang memadai daripada yang terbaik, terutama ketika makanan menipis dan waktu singkat, dan menjelaskan pertukaran itu: keputusan yang lebih cepat berisiko menghasilkan pilihan yang lebih buruk, sedangkan keputusan yang lebih lambat berisiko kehabisan makanan sebelum pilihan apa pun dibuat."},{"id":"IELR40","subtes":"Reading","teks":3,"soal":"Scientists study this behaviour because it shows","opsi":["that bees are more intelligent than human beings.","that leaders are unnecessary in every human group.","how a group with no central control can reach a sound decision through simple rules and independent checks.","how honey is produced inside the hive.","that swarms always find the best possible site."],"kunci":2,"bahas":"Bacaan menyatakan ilmuwan mempelajari perilaku itu karena menunjukkan bagaimana kelompok tanpa kendali pusat dapat mencapai keputusan yang baik melalui aturan sederhana dan pemeriksaan independen. Bacaan tidak mengklaim lebah lebih cerdas daripada manusia, tidak menggeneralisasi ke semua kelompok manusia, tidak menjelaskan produksi madu, dan mengakui sistem itu tidak sempurna."}]};
